# `while (at) { c = this.order(key, at.key); ..`: the loop reads its callee out of a field of `this` in every turn. The program stores two closures into that field, both by its name, at construction: Link holds the set; it is no proof (unseen code might store another), so it is an expectation to be tested at the run. A callee that stands in front of a loop was told apart there already and the loop built once for each closure; a callee read IN the loop was passed over: one head, a call by value in every turn. Now the field is read once in front of the loop and the closure told apart there: a loop with `a - b` in line, a loop with the comparison of texts in line, and the loop as written for whatever else the field holds.
# f1 = Shelf, f2 = put, f3 = depth, f4 = (a, b) => a - b, f5 = the other
